A||N||14||fropuff||How should I do it???||||1||
Z||000000||fropuff||10-26-2000||09:59 PM||||Hey, I've been thinkin' that I want to put a 10" sub on the floor in the passenger side footwell.  I want to build a custom box that JUST fits, as not to sacrifice too much space.  I want the sub to face the floor, with it up a few inches so it won't distort too much.  What I'm wondering is:  if I did build a box for a 10", would I build a solid box?  I know I sound retarded, but should I put a "screen" on so it can be "heard"?  Ported, I think it's called?  I am NOT a stereo expert by the way.  Since I'm not having it face the seat, do I need to build a solid box?  I hope you know what I'm talking about.  Pictures would help a lot if anyone has any.  Thanks a lot.  Sorry for the vague description.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||zeusrekning||10-26-2000||10:27 PM||||i'm helping madrox build a seald box for a 10" sub in the pass. footwell.The part that goes back farthest (next to the console is about 14" wide by 11"high i think. You can cram a ten in there and make it look pretty well ,I think,. you will want a sub that requiers a small box volume. I prefer sealed boxes for ease of building. Ported boxes (have ports/tubes in them) require a bigger box. From what i have been told a ported box will give you more sound with less power and a sealed box will give you tighter (better)bass. A sealed box builds up pressure in the box which causes the speaker coil not to bottom out against the magnet when the speaker gets pulled back in.( a speaker goes &lt; this way and &gt; that way). If you have a high power amp and a cheaper sub, when the sub gets pulled back there is no pressure to slow it down and stop it from bottoming out.<BR>Also a ported box needs to be tuned(the ports must be a certain diameter and length depending on sub, volume, and box shape.<BR>Take this info if you want it. But one fact i will stand beside. EVERYONE has different beliefs in car audio.<BR>i'll keep you posted on the box were building if you want. <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carolinafieros.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||10-26-2000||10:32 PM||||So I don't need to get any screens with the method that I'm using?  I was thinking of putting it in that farthest-back area.  I hope it works.  SO: I want to buy a 10", build a SEALED box?  Will the method I described work?  Thanks. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||zeusrekning||10-29-2000||09:00 PM||||i am not sure what you mean by a screen. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||10-29-2000||09:16 PM||||I had a friend build me a custom 10" fiberglass enclosure that only takes away an inch or so of passenger foot room.<P>You can email him at FieroGTguy@aol.com<P>It will cost you though...<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||14
Z||000005||stimpy||10-29-2000||10:10 PM||||I love my sub enclosure. And someday I may even get a pic scanned so I can post it. (sheesh! I've been waiting months for that d&mn pic. WebTV sucks!) <BR>As far as it costing a lot to build a fiberglass enclosure, I spent less then $30 on materials, an that includes the waffle grill and terminal cup.  I made it myself in about a week or so, and it was the first thing I had ever made with fiberglass. As far as firing it at the floor, I'm not sure what that will do for you, other then take up more space. My enclosure only comes out as far as the footrest on the side of the footwell, plenty of legroom left.  1FST2M6 provided my inspiration with his system, maybe he will post his pic again.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fropuff||10-29-2000||10:30 PM||||I wasn't really planning on firing it at the floor, I just wanted to see what someone else thought.  Since I was going to go with Rodney Dickman's sub kit, I was going to have to buy a wiring harness, enclosure, etc.  This way, I will have to do the same, but it will be better and I can make it my way!  That's better.  Since they only stick out about 5 inches, it won't take up that much space, if I can get it right.  Who really has their feet all the way jammed into the floorboard anyway?  Even if they did, I would yell at them.  I don't really care if it takes a few inches off their space.  I can either take it out, or they will have to learn to live with it.  With the seat all the way back, it should be fine.  Stimpy:  is your box ported or enclosed?  Thanks.  Any more info. would be greatly appreciated.  Thanks again. <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||10-29-2000||10:35 PM||||By the way, I found 1FST2M6s sub box at:  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg</A>   This is what I was thinking about, just making the sub a little more hidden with a grill.  I will probably try to find some OEM carpet to put on it as well.  I think it will turn out AOK.  <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 10-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||10-29-2000||10:44 PM||||fropuff, I looked through archives and found my build-up diary. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002401.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002401.html</A> <BR>I found a fiero in the junkyard, cut out the carpet behind the seat and tacked it to the face of my enclosure. Glue didn't work to well, and the nails are invisible under the loop of the carpet. Note: try to find capeting that is a waste in the front. I'd hate to be at the junkyard and find good carpet just to find out some jamoke cut a bg hole in it behind the seat.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||10-29-2000||11:37 PM||||It would be easy to put a box in the passenger side foot well. I have a picture of one and ill scan it tomarrow at work. It would sound like crap if you had it firing into the floor. Like Stimpy said in another thread somwhere, you could put an 8" in the stock location under the dash, its a pain though. You have to modify the dash, as well as taking it out of the car. I have heard of someone putting a 10" under the dash in the stock location though. If you are going to put one in the footwell, I would probally reccomend the ported option. It would sound beter. The way I am thinking of it, you could put a 10" sub in there and have a port tube to the top right of the sub. Also, like others have already said, it would have to be tuned.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit.gif"> <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000010||Philphine||10-30-2000||12:27 PM||||i used an 8 where most used a 10 for more foot room, but now i'm trying to add more subs to be satisfied with my bass. if i do another car (possibly my indy) i will probaly go with a 10 in a bigger box.||||reg||1
Z||000011||olympic||10-30-2000||02:00 PM||||  You'll definitely want to go with a sealed enclosure if space is a concern.  Many modern 10" subs will work in as little as 0.25 cubic feet in a sealed box(kicker solo-baric for example).  Ported enclosures are usually 50%-100% larger.<P>    An install shop(or yourself) would be able to make a molded enclosure out of fiberglass and bondo that would make maximum use of any extra space.  It would be expensive to pay someone to make it though as it is a labor intensive job.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||10-30-2000||02:19 PM||||I guess its just personal prefernce, or how much work you want to put into it. I would definatly go ported, but sealed would work fair also. Sorry, I forgot the picture I was going to scan, but Ill get it tomarrow. The idea I have in my head for a ported box would take up 5" of footroom at the front, and would vary slightly depending on which sub you wanted to use. Another thing you have to take into account is the mounting depth of the sub. Most 10's are 4.5"-6", maybe 7" with select brands. Theres always the option to do what I did and put a 10" sub in the spare tire well and port the bandpass enclosure into the passenger side floor, just like where you are wanting to put your sub. It would take no foot room at all, and your going to get excelent performance with a bandpass box, over any other designs:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero5_1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero6_1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero7.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/speaker_3.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
